---
description: Bash scripts that fail loudly and expand safely
globs: "**/*.sh"
alwaysApply: false
---

# Shell scripts

- Start every script with `#!/usr/bin/env bash`.
- Enable strict mode right after the shebang: `set -euo pipefail`.
- Quote every expansion: `"$var"`, `"$(cmd)"`, `"${array[@]}"`.
- Use `$(...)` for command substitution, never backticks.
- Never use `eval`: build commands as arrays and run `"${command[@]}"`.
- Send error messages to stderr (`>&2`) and exit non-zero.

Why: without strict mode a failed command or an unset variable lets the script carry on with bad state; unquoted expansions split and glob on whitespace; backticks don't nest and hide quoting bugs; `eval` runs whatever ends up in the string.

## Checks
- enforced-by-hook: shellcheck
- enforced-by: shell-strict-mode
- enforced-by: no-backtick-substitution
- enforced-by: no-eval
- enforcement: llm-review
